hi there steve here uh i've now  completed a week of turkish i want to  talk a bit about my experience my first  week with turkish  as part of a sort of a description of  how i see the language learning  process all right i've used various  metaphors in the past i've referred to  the uh the idea of language learning as  sort of a snowball that the more words  you pick up the more words you can  acquire that certainly becomes true  in that second stage i've talked about  three stages the first stage where we  struggle to get a sense of this strange  language which is just noise where we  it's no longer strange it becomes a  little bit familiar to us that's the  first stage the second stage where we  start to accumulate more and more words  until we get comfortable in the language  then the third stage is once we are  totally able to enjoy the language we  still make mistakes we still have things  we don't understand but we are enjoying  genuinely interesting content movies  whatever discussions and we are  continuing to infinity in terms of  trying to improve as much as we can in  the language so i'm very much at the  first stage so if i look at my first  week of activity and and remember again  that the key thing in language learning  is activity it's it's interacting with  the language don't worry about what you  achieve don't worry about what you  remember just make sure you engage with  the language so that's what i've been  doing  so if i look at my statistics at link  for example for the past week i think  i'm up to over 500 known words  i have saved i think two and a half  thousand links  and of those i have learned some 350 of  them so that the remaining links that i  created are still yellow links on my  lesson page because i have i keep on  seeing them in  new stories but i don't yet feel that i  know them i feel i know 500 of which 350  were learned presumably the other 200  were picked up because they were similar  to other words or they were words that  came from another language  french or english or arabic or persian  and i felt that i knew them without  having to look them up  i have read i think 25 000 words  sometimes reading the same lesson twice  but it's all reading so i have been very  active i had one very active day  two days ago where i have where i  created a thousand links i have read the  first 53 stories  in the mini  mini story series  but if i look at these on my lesson page  the early stories are largely white or  pale yellow as these words are gradually  becoming known  the more recent stories are  that i have read are full of  dark yellow words and phrases that i  have  saved that i have looked up but that i  don't yet know and as i progress into  new stories it'll be very heavy to blue  30 40 50  of the words in the news stories will be  stories that i haven't yet met on link  so that that the the jigsaw puzzle and  so far as the first five six seven  stories uh is concerned  are concerned there the jigsaw puzzle is  starting to show me some shape because  once the page is white that means that  in my mind these words  are clear  now  the next stage of course is to get to  where i can listen to these stories and  understand them and of course i'm not  yet there so i can read stories story 10  story 15 i understand it i can listen  and read i can go through listening  sentence by sentence and reading it and  understanding it  listening while reading but when i go  off and listen  without the help of the text i have  trouble understanding it so that's kind  of my process after one week of turkish  on link that's sort of the level of  activity i have exposed myself to the  language  i don't understand that well but i  understand when i read now one thing  about the language learning process it's  very much a matter of doing some of this  and some of that going forward coming  back and i mention that because  you know it's it's good to vary things  like if i have a very heavy day where i  saved a thousand links and i plowed  forward into 10 or 15 news stories  the next day i'm a little tired of doing  that so to vary things  i have a book  okay complete  turkish it has audio i can't listen to  the audio because it's full of english  you know  john and mary go to the grocer and order  bread and great long explanation in  english of the story i don't like  listening to that because i like  listening to the target language many  times so i can't listen to that stuff  but it is interesting because they start  to introduce for example  verbs  and  whatever they say about the verbs  are things that i have already  experienced so there is no  urgency to introduce verbs or anything  at any particular time you can always go  back to it similarly with basic  greetings i'm not necessarily very good  at saying hello how are you in turkish  and in this book they introduced very  early going to the store to buy bread or  olives and stuff like that  i haven't necessarily come across these  words in my many stories however  with the intensive exposure that i have  had to the turkish language through what  i have done in the first week when i go  into this book and i look up or i read  their explanation about how verbs work  or how cases work  uh or how to say hello how are you good  evening good morning it becomes a lot  easier for me because i have had this  exposure through my  activities at link so the point i want  to make is that language learning to me  the process is not one of building  blocks you learn this and once you have  that you learn the next step and the  next step it's more a matter of exposing  yourself to a lot of different elements  in the language constantly being able to  go back and review things that you have  forgotten or things that might be  considered basic but when they are first  introduced you can't you aren't yet in a  position to absorb them but if you have  simply allowed yourself like when i plow  into the new stories where there are a  lot of new and unknown words i kind of  feel as if i'm cross-country skiing and  i'm  and let's say i'm going around this  track twice so the first time i'm  actually having to create the track  because the snow is deep and i'm skiing  and creating a track but the second time  i come through there i already have a  track and i'm going more  easily more smoothly so  this is it you have to kind of go around  the track many many times picking up  different things each time maybe if i'm  going through the forest i'll notice a  different tree each time and that is the  process so i don't know if i make any  sense but i just wanted to stress the  fact that the key  aspect of language learning is to be  active to interact with the language to  push yourself to explore and you can  always go back later and pick up other  bits and pieces when you are motivated  to do so thanks for listening bye for  now  you
 

